WEBVTT

00:00:00.680 --> 00:00:04.690
A knitter is asking about knitting
swatches using DesignaKnit.

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:08.520
She has a Silver Reed electronic machine,
and with these machines,

00:00:08.586 --> 00:00:14.170
in order to knit in pattern for her
swatches, she needs to use DesignaKnit.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:18.050
She asked how she should set
the gauge to knit her swatch.

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:21.490
The answer is, it doesn't matter.

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:23.490
Let's explore why.

00:00:23.560 --> 00:00:27.370
If you're using Stitch Designer and you're
creating a new stitch pattern,

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:31.490
it's important to set your gauge
similar to what you'll be knitting.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:37.130
For example, these hearts knit at this
gauge are a little bit distorted

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:41.640
from knitting them at this gauge.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.360
In Stitch Designer,
DesignaKnit uses your entered gauge

00:00:45.426 --> 00:00:48.250
to display your stitch pattern.

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:53.410
It's up to you to design the hearts
so they look good at your gauge.

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:57.240
In designing stitch patterns,
there are no calculations like

00:00:57.306 --> 00:00:59.130
in designing garment pieces.

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:04.400
So DesignaKnit it uses the gauge
to preview how a stitch pattern will look.

00:01:04.867 --> 00:01:07.890
To knit a swatch, we'll
use Interactive knitting.

00:01:07.960 --> 00:01:12.160
For this example, we'll use this
pretty hearts stitch pattern.

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:16.370
Open Interactive Knitting and tell DesignaKnit that you want to use

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:18.090
just the stitch pattern.

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:21.400
Now, we're not going to be
using the shape file.

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:26.240
For a standard gauge swatch,
it's common to cast on 60 stitches

00:01:26.306 --> 00:01:30.040
and knit for 80 rows
so that you can measure 40 stitches gauges

00:01:30.106 --> 00:01:35.440
in 60 rows and use a green
gauge ruler to measure.

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:40.520
Of course, you could choose to knit
your swatch any size you like.

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:48.920
DesignaKnit is going to display
a preview of the hearts as they will

00:01:48.986 --> 00:01:52.450
look at the gauge that is currently set.

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:59.600
Now, if you change the gauge, you
can see the changes in this preview.

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:04.930
However, changing the gauge is not
impacting the number of stitches

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:06.920
and rows you're knitting.

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:12.080
No matter what gauge you set,
the display will change, but the stitches

00:02:12.146 --> 00:02:15.170
and rows will remain the same.

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:20.890
You will establish your gauge by knitting
60 stitches and 80 rows,

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:24.760
and it may look very different than
the display in Interactive Knitting.

